// REQUIRES: asan
// Guards the sanitizer harness itself rather than any generated code: does an
// ASan build actually link and load the *sanitized* runtime?
//
// Every other case here answers "does instrumented Reussir code misbehave",
// and every one of them passes just as happily against the ordinary runtime —
// whose mimalloc heap the sanitizers cannot see. That is a suite that reports
// green while checking substantially less than its name claims, and it is the
// state this suite was in on Windows: the staged runtime was renamed while its
// import library still named the original, so tests silently loaded the
// uninstrumented build (see the runtime-selection notes in
// crates/reussir-rt/CMakeLists.txt).
//
// This case cannot pass in that state. It plants a use-after-free in memory
// the runtime allocated: reported when the sanitized runtime is in the
// process, silent when it is not.
//
// It compiles no Reussir source — the `.rr` suffix is what makes lit collect
// it, and `Inputs/` is where the suite keeps its C drivers.

// RUN: %cc %asan_flags %S/Inputs/runtime_allocator_uaf.c %reussir_rt_asan -o %t.exe %rpath_flag %rpath_san_flag %extra_sys_libs
// DIAGNOSTIC, REMOVE ONCE CLASSIFIED: the x86_64 Linux CI child exits
// nonzero with no output at all. `%not` cannot distinguish a signal death
// from an early return, and piping into FileCheck discards the evidence
// either way. Run it directly, record the raw status, print everything,
// then fail on purpose so lit shows what was captured.
// RUN: %asan_env ASAN_OPTIONS=verbosity=1:abort_on_error=0:halt_on_error=1 %t.exe > %t.diag 2>&1; echo "RAW_EXIT=$?" >> %t.diag; cat %t.diag; false
// RUN-DISABLED: %asan_env %not %t.exe 2>&1 | %FileCheck %s

// CHECK: ERROR: AddressSanitizer: heap-use-after-free
// CHECK: READ of size 1
